  sudo apt install cmake bison flex re2c ruby

  # For autotools build (if using legacy)
  sudo apt install automake autoconf libtool

  🎯 Recommended Workflow

  1. Use CMake - it's faster, more reliable, and cross-platform
  2. Set LMNTAL_HOME environment variable
  3. Run ./build-cmake.sh - that's it!

  The CMake system I created handles all the code generation (parsers, config files, etc.)
  automatically at build time.

> what's the CMake alternative to 'make clean' ?

● Here are the CMake alternatives to make clean:

  🧹 CMake Clean Commands

  1. Clean Build Files (like make clean)

  cd /home/ueda/slim-cmake/build-test
  make clean
  # OR
  cmake --build . --target clean

  2. Complete Clean Build (recommended)

  cd /home/ueda/slim-cmake

  # Remove entire build directory
  rm -rf build

  # Start fresh
  mkdir build mkdir build-test && cd build-testmkdir build-test && cd build-test cd build
  cmake ..
  make -j$(nproc)

  3. Using the Build Script

  cd /home/ueda/slim-cmake

  # The build script creates a fresh build directory each time
  ./build-cmake.sh

  📁 What Each Method Cleans

  | Method            | What it removes                                  |
  |-------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
  | make clean        | Object files, executables, but keeps CMake cache |
  | rm -rf build | Everything - complete fresh start                |
  | ./build-cmake.sh  | Creates new build directory automatically        |

  🎯 Best Practice

  For CMake projects, the cleanest approach is:
  # Complete clean
  rm -rf build

  # Rebuild
  ./build-cmake.sh

  This ensures no stale cache files or configuration issues, which is especially useful when:
  - Changing CMake options
  - After modifying CMakeLists.txt files
  - Troubleshooting build issues

  The out-of-source build approach (separate build directory) makes this very safe and fast!

