> what should I do when I build a new git branch and go there and work there ?

● When creating and working on a new git branch in the SLIM-cmake project, here's the complete
  workflow:

  🌿 New Git Branch Workflow

  1. Create and Switch to New Branch

  cd /home/ueda/slim-cmake

  # Create and switch to new branch
  git checkout -b feature/my-new-feature
  # OR
  git branch feature/my-new-feature
  git checkout feature/my-new-feature

  # Verify you're on the new branch
  git branch

  2. Clean Build Environment

  # Remove any existing build artifacts from previous branch
  rm -rf build

  # Ensure environment is set
  export LMNTAL_HOME=/path/to/your/lmntal/compiler

  3. Build on New Branch

  # Fresh build on the new branch
  ./build-cmake.sh

  # OR manual build
  mkdir build && cd build
  cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
  make -j$(nproc)

  4. Development Workflow

  # Make your code changes...
  # Edit source files, CMakeLists.txt, etc.

  # Incremental builds (if small changes)
  cd build-test && make -j$(nproc)

  # Complete rebuild (if major changes or issues)
  rm -rf build && ./build-cmake.sh

  # Test your changes
  ./build-test/src/slim --help
  cd build-test && make test

  5. When Switching Between Branches

  # Switch to another branch
  git checkout main
  # OR
  git checkout other-feature-branch

  # IMPORTANT: Clean build after branch switch
  rm -rf build
  ./build-cmake.sh

  🔧 Branch-Specific Scenarios

  If you modify CMakeLists.txt files:

  # Always do complete clean rebuild
  rm -rf build
  ./build-cmake.sh

  If you add new source files:

  # 1. Update appropriate CMakeLists.txt
  vim src/CMakeLists.txt  # or src/vm/CMakeLists.txt, etc.

  # 2. Clean rebuild
  rm -rf build
  ./build-cmake.sh

  For different build configurations per branch:

  # Debug build for development branch
  ./build-cmake.sh --debug

  # Release build for performance testing
  ./build-cmake.sh

  # Custom build
  mkdir build-custom && cd build-custom
  cmake .. -DENABLE_PROFILE=ON -DENABLE_DEVEL=ON
  make -j$(nproc)

  📋 Best Practices

  ✅ Always do after branch switch:

  1. rm -rf build (clean all build artifacts)
  2. ./build-cmake.sh (fresh build)
  3. Test the build works

  ✅ Before committing:

  # Ensure clean build works
  rm -rf build && ./build-cmake.sh

  # Run tests
  cd build-test && make test

  # Commit your changes
  git add .
  git commit -m "Your commit message"

  ✅ When merging branches:

  # After merge, always clean rebuild
  git merge feature/my-feature
  rm -rf build
  ./build-cmake.sh

  ⚠️ Why Clean Builds Matter

  CMake caches configuration between branches, so different branches with different:
  - CMakeLists.txt changes
  - New/removed files
  - Different compile flags

  Can cause issues if you don't clean rebuild after switching branches.

  Simple rule: New branch = clean build!

